      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1765420064076,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Support for Isolation & security:\n    - read only file system \n    - Explicit Timeout: TLE \n    - Explicit Memory & CPU bound: MLE \n\n2) scaling to support competition: \n    - vertical scaling \n    - Dynamic horizontal scaling \n    - Dynamic horizontal scaling with queue: we \nadd submission to queue-> worker take-up -> on completion notifies app server to\nupdate the status. One important thing to note is that the introduction of the\nqueue has made the system asynchronous. This means that the API server will no\nlonger be able to return the results of the submission immediately. Instead, the user\nwill need to poll the server for the results. We introduce a new endpoint, say GET\n/check/:id, that is polled every second by the client to check if the submission has\nbeen processed. It simply looks up the submission in the database and returns the\nresults if they are available or returns a \"processing\" message if not.\n    - A proposed solution is to run only about 10% of test cases during the contest,\nshowing participants partial scores in real time. After the contest ends, organizers\ncan run all submissions on the full set of test cases to produce final, official results.\nThis approach reduces infrastructure costs, though it delays final standings—similar\nto how Codeforces manages its large-scale contests.\n    ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Support for Isolation & security:\n    - read only file system \n    - Explicit Timeout: TLE \n    - Explicit Memory & CPU bound: MLE \n\n2) scaling to support competition: \n    - vertical scaling \n    - Dynamic horizontal scaling \n    - Dynamic horizontal scaling with queue: we \nadd submission to queue-> worker take-up -> on completion notifies app server to update the status. One important thing to note is that the introduction of the queue has made the system asynchronous. This means that the API server will no longer be able to return the results of the submission immediately. Instead, the user will need to poll the server for the results. We introduce a new endpoint, say GET /check/:id, that is polled every second by the client to check if the submission has been processed. It simply looks up the submission in the database and returns the results if they are available or returns a \"processing\" message if not.\n    - A proposed solution is to run only about 10% of test cases during the contest, showing participants partial scores in real time. After the contest ends, organizers can run all submissions on the full set of test cases to produce final, official results.\nThis approach reduces infrastructure costs, though it delays final standings—similar to how Codeforces manages its large-scale contests.\n    ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
